<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

$q = static fn(string $difficulty, string $stem, string $answer, array $distractors, string $explanation, string $competency, string $scenario): array => [
    'difficulty'=>$difficulty, 'question'=>$stem, 'answer'=>$answer, 'distractors'=>$distractors,
    'explanation'=>$explanation, 'tested_competency'=>$competency, 'scenario'=>$scenario,
];

return [
    'ece-responsive-language-scaffolding'=>[
        $q('easy','During a nature-sorting activity, a child explains in Ilokano why two leaves belong together. Which response best supports language and concept learning?','Acknowledge the explanation, restate its idea, and add a related classroom-language word while inviting the child to continue.',['Ask the child to wait until able to give the explanation only in English.','Translate the answer for the class, then move on without returning to the child.','Treat the language choice as evidence that the child has not understood the sorting idea.'],'The kindergarten guide recognizes the child’s first language as a resource. Restating meaning and adding vocabulary supports both participation and language development.','Value first language while extending oral language through responsive interaction.','leaf-sorting-conversation'),
        $q('medium','While retelling a familiar story, a learner moves between Filipino and a home language and loses the group’s attention. What is the strongest teaching move?','Invite the learner to continue the retelling, then model one useful connecting phrase and ask a peer to build on the story idea.',['Require the learner to restart in one language before classmates may respond.','Replace the retelling with a vocabulary worksheet so the group stays on schedule.','Ask the family to stop using the home language during story time.'],'The child is communicating meaning. A modeled phrase and peer response extend oral language without treating multilingual expression as a deficit.','Use responsive interaction to scaffold oral narrative and turn-taking.','multilingual-story-retell'),
        $q('medium','A child understands a picture-based sorting task but rarely answers the teacher’s spoken questions in the classroom language. Which next step best checks understanding while supporting language?','Offer a choice to point, place an object, or answer with a familiar word, then model the classroom-language term in context.',['Repeat the same question louder until the child gives a spoken response.','Conclude that the child cannot sort until fluent classroom-language speech develops.','Give the child a separate easier sorting task without checking the intended concept.'],'A response mode can reveal the concept while the teacher builds classroom language in context; speech fluency is not the sorting target.','Separate language-access demands from the concept being checked.','nonverbal-response-language'),
        $q('medium','A caregiver shares a short home-language song that children use during a daily routine. How can the teacher use it to extend oral language appropriately?','Invite children to notice repeated sounds or actions, learn the song with family attribution, and connect its words to the routine.',['Use the song as a reward only after children complete English pronunciation drills.','Present the song as a generic tradition shared by all families in the class.','Avoid the song because home-language material does not belong in literacy learning.'],'A familiar song can connect sound, meaning, routine, and relationship; the teacher should attribute its source and avoid generalizing it.','Use familiar language experiences as contextual resources.','family-song-literacy'),
        $q('medium','During a discussion, one child watches closely and points to the object named by a classmate but does not answer aloud. What should the teacher infer?','The pointing is evidence of participation; offer a supported way to add an idea and observe language across other contexts.',['The child has mastered all vocabulary because the correct object was selected once.','The child does not understand because no spoken answer was produced.','The child should be moved away from group discussion until speech improves.'],'A single nonverbal response is useful evidence but does not establish complete mastery or lack of understanding; collect further evidence.','Interpret partial language evidence without overclaiming.','receptive-language-evidence'),
        $q('hard','A team plans a new unit because several children use unfamiliar words for local plants. Which evidence-based language plan is strongest?','Ask families about the children’s terms and experiences, connect them to accurate curriculum vocabulary, and use both during hands-on observation.',['Replace the children’s words with standardized labels before discussing the plants.','Assume each unfamiliar word refers to the same plant and prepare one translation list.','Delay the unit until all children use identical vocabulary for the plants.'],'Family knowledge can clarify meaning and connect language to experience; the teacher can add accurate curriculum terms without erasing children’s vocabulary.','Integrate family language knowledge with accurate vocabulary and shared inquiry.','local-plant-vocabulary'),
    ],
    'ece-emergent-number-sense'=>[
        $q('easy','At snack time, a child places one cup beside each of four classmates and notices one cup remains. What concept is the teacher best positioned to explore next?','One-to-one matching and whether the number of cups matches the number of classmates.',['The written form of four independent of any quantity.','A timed count of number words with no objects present.','A comparison of which child can distribute cups most quickly.'],'The routine makes one-to-one correspondence and quantity meaningful; the teacher can ask how the child noticed the extra cup.','Use concrete matching to build number and quantity understanding.','snack-distribution'),
        $q('medium','A child counts the same set of shells twice after the teacher spreads them farther apart, giving a different total each time. What is a useful next activity?','Let the child move each shell into a new row as it is counted, then compare the result with the original count.',['Ask the child to memorize the last total and repeat it without touching the shells.','Add more shells immediately so the child practices a larger sequence.','Show the correct total and ask the child to copy the numeral several times.'],'Moving each object once links the count word sequence to one-to-one correspondence and helps the child check a changing arrangement.','Connect number words to counted objects and quantity.','rearranged-shell-set'),
        $q('medium','Children make a sound pattern with clap, tap, clap, tap. Which prompt best advances pattern reasoning?','Ask what sound comes next and what part of the sequence repeats.',['Ask them to recite the number names while the pattern continues.','Tell them the next sound and have them repeat the whole sequence.','Ask which child made the loudest sound in the sequence.'],'Predicting and identifying the repeating unit makes the pattern structure explicit.','Notice, extend, and explain a repeating pattern.','sound-pattern'),
        $q('medium','A group measures a block tower with linking cubes and compares it with a second tower. Which procedure makes the comparison meaningful?','Use equal-sized cubes and begin each measurement at the same base level.',['Use whatever blocks are nearby and estimate from the tallest point.','Place cubes with gaps so learners can finish the measurement sooner.','Compare the number of colors rather than the units covering each tower.'],'A consistent unit and shared starting point make the measures comparable.','Use consistent nonstandard units to reason about length.','tower-measurement'),
        $q('medium','In a pretend market, children arrange picture cards for opening, shopping, paying, and closing. Which question integrates everyday numeracy and sequence?','Ask which event comes before payment and how many item cards are in the basket.',['Ask each child to write a clock time for every event before the play begins.','Ask which card looks most colorful and remove the other cards.','Ask the children to count the people rather than interpret the event order.'],'Order and count are meaningful within the play context, with concrete representations rather than unsupported clock notation.','Connect mathematical relations to an everyday context.','market-sequence'),
        $q('hard','The class has 12 buttons for sorting. One learner groups by color, another by size, and a third makes a repeating color pattern. What is the strongest teacher response?','Ask each learner to explain the rule used and invite the group to test whether every button fits that rule.',['Require everyone to use color because the first learner began that way.','Choose one learner’s arrangement as correct and have the others copy it.','Count the buttons aloud and stop the activity before comparing the groupings.'],'Multiple attributes can support different valid classifications. Explanation and testing make the rule visible without imposing a single arbitrary strategy.','Compare classification rules and check whether each object meets them.','button-classification'),
    ],
    'ece-dialogic-literacy-practice'=>[
        $q('easy','During a shared picture book, a child points to a character’s empty bowl. Which prompt best invites meaning-making?','Ask what the picture suggests the character may do next and what clue supports the idea.',['Ask the child to repeat the title until it is memorized.','Name the next event yourself and ask the child to echo it.','Ask the child to identify every printed letter before discussing the picture.'],'An open prediction tied to a visual clue invites comprehension, language, and evidence-based thinking.','Use dialogic prompts to connect image clues with meaning.','picture-book-prediction'),
        $q('medium','A learner retells a story by naming the ending first, then the beginning. What is a useful next teacher prompt?','Invite the learner to place three picture cards in story order and explain how one event leads to another.',['Correct the child’s wording before asking about story events.','Ask the learner to copy a sentence from the last page.','Tell the learner to listen again without offering a way to revisit the story.'],'Picture sequencing gives the learner a concrete way to organize events and explain temporal relationships.','Use retelling and sequencing as evidence of comprehension.','story-sequence-retell'),
        $q('medium','Children recognize a familiar sign near the school gate but cannot read all of its words. How can the teacher use the sign for emergent literacy?','Discuss where the sign appears, what it communicates, and which print or symbol clues help identify it.',['Tell children that recognizing a sign is irrelevant until they can decode each word.','Cover the pictures and ask learners to memorize the sign’s exact text.','Use the sign only to practice handwriting its first letter.'],'Environmental print has meaning in context; noticing words, symbols, and location supports print awareness.','Connect environmental print to purpose and meaning.','school-gate-sign'),
        $q('medium','A child asks about an unfamiliar word in a story. Which response best supports vocabulary and comprehension?','Reread the surrounding sentence, connect the word to the illustration or action, and invite the child to explain a possible meaning.',['Give a dictionary definition and move immediately to the next page.','Ask the child to copy the word several times before hearing the story again.','Skip the word because vocabulary discussion interrupts reading.'],'Context, illustration, and discussion help the learner infer and use a new word meaningfully.','Build vocabulary through contextualized discussion.','unfamiliar-story-word'),
        $q('medium','A teacher wants to introduce rhyme using a familiar poem. Which activity is most appropriate for kindergarten learners?','Read the poem with rhythm, invite children to notice or supply a rhyming word, and discuss the sound pattern.',['Require every child to complete a written worksheet before hearing the poem.','Teach a list of rhyme definitions without connecting them to spoken words.','Ask children to identify the poem’s punctuation before listening to sound.'],'Hearing and playing with sound in a meaningful text develops phonological awareness without replacing experience with abstract drill.','Explore sound patterns through oral language and meaningful text.','poem-rhyme'),
        $q('hard','A learner draws several marks beside a picture and says the marks tell a grandparent what happened. What is a strong literacy response?','Ask the learner to explain the message, record the child’s words if useful, and discuss how marks and pictures carry meaning.',['Erase the marks and provide a tracing page with correct letter forms.','Tell the learner the message is not writing because it is not conventionally spelled.','Ask the learner to memorize the adult’s sentence and copy it exactly.'],'The child is using marks and images to communicate meaning. The teacher can value the intent and model conventional print without dismissing the message.','Recognize emergent writing as communication and extend print understanding.','emergent-message'),
    ],
    'ece-observation-to-responsive-planning'=>[
        $q('easy','Which note is the clearest observation record during block play?','“At 9:10, Mira placed three blocks in a row and said, ‘This is the bridge.’”',['“Mira is a natural engineer.”','“Mira was more advanced than the other children today.”','“Mira understands every idea about building.”'],'The record gives a time, action, and direct words without converting one event into a broad judgment.','Document observable actions before interpreting them.','objective-observation-note'),
        $q('medium','A child leaves the group most mornings just before clean-up. What should the teacher examine before drawing a conclusion?','Repeated observations of the transition, cues, noise, task demands, and the child’s response after different supports.',['Whether the child should lose a preferred activity for leaving the group.','Whether the child has a fixed attention problem based on this pattern alone.','Whether other children can be asked to bring the child back each morning.'],'A repeated pattern calls for examination of context and response to support, not a diagnosis or punishment based on one behavior.','Use contextual repeated evidence to guide assessment and planning.','clean-up-transition'),
        $q('medium','A portfolio includes one painting but no date, prompt, or learner explanation. What addition most improves its value for planning?','Add the date, activity context, relevant observation, and the learner’s own explanation of the work.',['Add a score comparing the painting with classmates’ work.','Replace the painting with a teacher-written summary of the child’s personality.','Add the child’s age and assume the painting shows age-level mastery.'],'Context and the learner’s explanation support interpretation and comparison over time.','Document context alongside work samples.','portfolio-context'),
        $q('medium','A kindergarten checklist flags that a learner may need further assessment. What is the teacher’s best next step?','Discuss the documented concern with the family and follow the school’s process for appropriate further assessment.',['Tell the family the checklist proves the learner has a developmental disorder.','Repeat the checklist until a different result appears and discard earlier records.','Use the checklist result to remove the learner from the activity.'],'The curriculum guide presents the checklist as a way to identify possible need for further assessment, not a diagnosis or exclusion decision.','Interpret screening/checklist evidence within its stated purpose.','checklist-follow-up'),
        $q('medium','A skill appears during outdoor play but not during a table task. Which interpretation best supports responsive teaching?','The difference may reflect context or task access, so gather more evidence and ask what features help the learner show the skill.',['The outdoor observation must be ignored because it was not a formal test.','The table result proves the learner has permanently lost the skill.','The learner should be assessed only in the setting with the lower score.'],'Performance can vary with materials, language, partners, and setting; compare conditions before planning.','Interpret variation in performance across contexts.','context-variation'),
        $q('hard','A teacher has two dated work samples, direct observations, and family input that a child enjoys sorting at home. Which planning choice best uses the evidence?','Offer a new sorting task with a modest challenge, record how the child explains the rule, and review whether participation grows.',['Repeat the same task until the child produces the teacher’s preferred answer.','Conclude that home performance guarantees the same classroom skill in every context.','Select a new activity based only on the child’s chronological age.'],'Multiple evidence sources suggest a relevant next experience, while the new observation checks learning in the classroom context.','Integrate evidence and plan a testable next step.','multi-source-planning'),
    ],
    'ece-guided-play-scaffolding'=>[
        $q('easy','Children build a pretend bus station with blocks. Which teacher action best extends the play without taking it over?','Ask where passengers will wait and offer a few picture route cards for children to use if they choose.',['Redesign the station while children watch so it matches the teacher’s model.','Pause the play and require a worksheet about buses.','Assign each child a fixed role before asking what they want to build.'],'An open question and optional material extend thinking while preserving child choice and play direction.','Scaffold child-initiated play without replacing it.','pretend-bus-station'),
        $q('medium','A child repeatedly rolls a ball down a ramp and watches how far it travels. What is a useful guided-play prompt?','Ask what might make the ball travel farther and invite the child to change one ramp feature to test the idea.',['Tell the child the correct result and end the exploration.','Add several new objects at once so the child cannot compare conditions.','Require the child to copy a written prediction before touching the ramp.'],'A single-variable change supports prediction and inquiry while the child remains active in play.','Extend exploration through prediction and comparison.','ramp-play-inquiry'),
        $q('medium','Several children use toy animals to create a rescue story but do not include a child who wants to join. What is the teacher’s best intervention?','Offer an open invitation to add a role or resource to the story and help the group consider how it could fit.',['Assign the child the lead role without asking and require the others to agree.','Stop the story and move the group to a different activity.','Tell the child to wait until the group finishes its play.'],'A flexible invitation supports entry and peer planning without taking control of the children’s story.','Use play scaffolding to support social inclusion.','rescue-story-entry'),
        $q('medium','A child is sorting pretend food by color. Which prompt encourages deeper thinking while respecting the play?','Ask whether the child can find another way to group the foods and explain the new rule.',['Tell the child to sort only by the categories printed on a worksheet.','Move the foods into the teacher’s preferred groups without discussion.','Ask the child to count every item aloud before any further choice.'],'The prompt expands classification and explanation while allowing the child’s own direction.','Use questions to extend concept depth during play.','pretend-food-sorting'),
        $q('medium','A teacher has modeled how to attach a cardboard roof, and a child now completes the steps without help. What should happen next?','Reduce prompting and observe whether the child can adapt the construction when a new material is offered.',['Continue giving the same instruction before every action.','End all construction opportunities because the child has finished the model.','Ask another learner to complete the next step for the child.'],'Fading support and testing transfer help the teacher see whether the child can apply the idea independently.','Adjust scaffolding in response to observed competence.','cardboard-roof-scaffold'),
        $q('hard','A play center has become quiet and repetitive, but several children still engage with the same props. What is the most defensible teacher move?','Observe what the children are trying to do, then introduce one relevant prop or question that opens a new possibility.',['Replace all the materials immediately with a teacher-designed task.','Assume repeated play has no learning value and close the center.','Ask children to complete a separate activity because the center is quiet.'],'The teacher should use observation to decide whether a small extension would deepen the children’s current interest.','Use play evidence to judge whether to extend or wait.','quiet-play-center'),
    ],
    'ece-home-language-partnership'=>[
        $q('easy','A caregiver shares that a child enjoys counting market items in a home language. How can the teacher use this knowledge?','Invite the child to count familiar objects in that language and connect the quantity to classroom number words.',['Ask the caregiver to stop the home counting routine during the school year.','Use the information to assign the child a lower number task.','Assume the child can count every classroom object in the same way without checking.'],'Family knowledge offers a bridge to the concept; the teacher still observes how the child uses it in the classroom.','Build continuity between home knowledge and classroom learning.','family-counting-routine'),
        $q('medium','A caregiver cannot attend an in-person classroom event but wants to share the child’s favorite story. What is a respectful partnership response?','Offer a flexible way to share the story or its key details and invite the caregiver to choose what is workable.',['Record the caregiver as uninvolved because the event was missed.','Require the caregiver to attend another event before using the story.','Use a story found online and tell the child it came from home.'],'Partnership should allow realistic two-way communication rather than making one event the test of family engagement.','Offer flexible reciprocal family communication.','family-event-barrier'),
        $q('medium','A child uses a familiar household routine to explain a sequencing activity. What should the teacher do with that contribution?','Ask the child to describe the routine, connect it to before-and-after language, and invite classmates to compare sequences.',['Replace the routine with a school example because home experiences are private.','Assume all families follow the same routine and teach it as universal.','Correct the child’s sequence before learning how the routine works.'],'A familiar routine can support temporal language while the teacher avoids assuming it represents every family.','Use family experience as a context without generalizing it.','home-routine-sequencing'),
        $q('medium','A family describes a child’s interest in boat travel, while the teacher is planning a unit on local weather. Which plan uses the information well?','Invite the family’s optional examples and use them to develop a weather observation question relevant to the child.',['Change the whole unit to boat travel without considering its learning goals.','Ignore the interest because families should not influence classroom planning.','Ask the child to bring expensive materials from home for the class.'],'Family knowledge can make learning relevant while the teacher maintains curricular goals and avoids burdening the family.','Use family knowledge to contextualize planned learning.','weather-interest'),
        $q('medium','A family reports that a child communicates more confidently through gestures at home. What is the best teacher response?','Ask which gestures are meaningful, observe how the child communicates in class, and plan consistent ways to recognize messages.',['Tell the family the child must stop gesturing to build speech.','Use gestures at home as the sole communication plan without checking how they work in other settings.','Ask peers to interpret every gesture without checking with the child.'],'The family’s insight can inform observation and planning; the teacher should confirm meaning with the child and family.','Use family insight as evidence while maintaining learner communication agency.','gesture-communication'),
        $q('hard','A child’s family shares a personal routine that could enrich a class discussion, but the child is uncomfortable when it is mentioned publicly. What should the teacher do?','Respect the child’s response, avoid sharing private details, and use another classroom context for the same learning goal.',['Share the routine anyway because the family first offered it.','Ask classmates to persuade the child to discuss the routine.','Remove the learning goal because the child declined to share personal information.'],'Family contribution does not override the child’s dignity and privacy; the learning goal can be met through another context.','Balance family partnership with the child’s privacy and choice.','private-family-routine'),
    ],
    'ece-peer-social-emotional-coregulation'=>[
        $q('easy','A child becomes upset when a caregiver leaves at arrival. Which response best supports co-regulation?','Stay close, name the feeling gently, and guide the child toward a predictable settling routine.',['Tell the child to stop crying before joining the class.','Leave the child alone until the emotion has passed.','Promise that the caregiver will return at a specific time the teacher cannot verify.'],'A calm relationship and predictable routine help the child regain participation without dismissing the feeling or making an unsupported promise.','Use responsive routines to support regulation.','arrival-separation'),
        $q('medium','Two children reach for the same paint tray and begin arguing. What is the best teaching response?','Acknowledge both interests and coach them to choose a turn-taking or shared-use plan.',['Remove the paint for the rest of the class period.','Choose one child as the permanent owner of the tray.','Tell the children that the louder one may use it first.'],'The teacher can support language, self-regulation, and a workable peer solution while keeping access to the activity.','Teach conflict language and collaborative problem-solving.','shared-paint-tray'),
        $q('medium','Several learners become unsettled each time the class switches from play to story. Which environmental support is most useful to try?','Give a consistent transition cue and preview what will happen next before the change.',['Add an unexpected timed race to make the transition exciting.','Remove the play materials without warning so the change is immediate.','Require learners to sit silently before explaining the next activity.'],'A predictable cue and preview reduce uncertainty and make the routine easier to follow.','Use consistent transition cues to support participation.','play-to-story-transition'),
        $q('medium','A learner becomes angry after losing a simple game and pushes the pieces away. What should the teacher teach after ensuring safety?','Help the learner name the frustration and practice a respectful way to request another turn or pause.',['Require an apology before discussing what happened.','Tell the learner that winning is unimportant and restart the game.','Remove the learner from all future games.'],'Naming the feeling and rehearsing a useful communication response teaches a replacement skill and supports re-entry.','Teach emotional language and a safe next action.','game-loss-frustration'),
        $q('medium','A group repeatedly leaves one child out of a pretend-play role. What is a constructive teacher move?','Observe the interaction, support the children in planning roles, and check whether each learner can contribute meaningfully.',['Assign the excluded child a role without asking what they want.','Tell the group to resolve it alone and leave the area.','Replace the play with individual seatwork for the whole group.'],'Teacher support can improve peer inclusion while respecting the children’s ideas and observing whether the change works.','Promote peer participation and reflect on the effect of support.','peer-role-exclusion'),
        $q('hard','A learner often uses a quiet corner during noisy group activities, then returns to participate. How should the teacher interpret the routine?','Treat it as information about what supports regulation, ask what the learner needs, and plan a respectful return to participation.',['Label it as avoidance and remove access to the quiet space.','Assume the child should remain in the corner for the whole activity.','Require the learner to explain the choice to the group each time.'],'A quiet space can support regulation when it is not punitive or isolating; the learner’s voice and re-entry matter.','Use environmental support to sustain agency and participation.','quiet-space-return'),
    ],
    'ece-inclusive-participation-design'=>[
        $q('easy','A learner finds a small paintbrush hard to grip during an art activity. Which adjustment best preserves the learning goal of exploring color?','Offer a larger-handled brush or another safe applicator and let the learner choose how to apply the color.',['Remove the learner from painting and ask them to watch.','Complete the painting for the learner so the class stays together.','Change the activity to a handwriting task for every child.'],'The adjustment changes access to the tool while retaining the color-exploration goal.','Adapt materials while keeping the intended learning aim.','paintbrush-grip'),
        $q('medium','A class discussion requires children to answer aloud, but one learner can show the relevant idea by arranging picture cards. What should the teacher do?','Accept the card sequence as evidence when oral speech is not the target, then invite an optional explanation.',['Insist that the learner answer aloud before the class may continue.','Give the learner unrelated coloring work during the discussion.','Count the sequence as incorrect because it was not spoken.'],'A response mode should match the learning target; card arrangement can show understanding when speech is not being assessed.','Offer an accessible response mode without changing the concept target.','picture-card-response'),
        $q('medium','A crowded shelf narrows the path to a popular learning center. What should the teacher check first?','Rearrange the furniture to create a clear path and observe whether children can reach and use the center independently.',['Ask children to wait until the crowd becomes smaller before approaching.','Move the center farther away without checking the new route.','Tell the learner who has difficulty moving to choose a different center.'],'The environment is a potential barrier; a clear path supports access for more learners and should be checked in use.','Remove a physical participation barrier in the shared environment.','crowded-center-path'),
        $q('medium','Learners miss a multi-step instruction during a noisy activity. Which support is most likely to improve access?','Present the steps clearly with a simple visual cue and check understanding before practice begins.',['Repeat the same long instruction from across the room.','Ask peers to guess what each learner should do.','Reduce the learning goal without checking whether the directions caused the difficulty.'],'A clear representation and comprehension check address an instruction-access barrier without changing the task goal.','Make directions accessible and verify understanding.','multi-step-instruction'),
        $q('medium','A group project requires every child to speak to the class, though the intended competency is sorting materials by function. What is the best design revision?','Allow learners to show the group’s sorting through an arranged display or supported explanation while assessing the classification.',['Keep public speaking as a hidden requirement because it is convenient to score.','Have the teacher sort the materials and ask learners to watch.','Remove the classification task for learners who do not present orally.'],'The assessment should measure sorting if that is the stated competency; public speaking can be optional unless separately taught and assessed.','Align participation options with the actual learning target.','group-sorting-presentation'),
        $q('hard','During music and movement, one learner covers their ears when several instruments play at once but joins when the group uses a softer single rhythm. What should the teacher do?','Plan a predictable lower-volume entry and ask the learner what helps, while observing participation in the shared rhythm task.',['Require the learner to remain in the loudest part until they become used to it.','Exclude the learner from all music activities because of the reaction.','Conclude that the learner dislikes music and change their curriculum.'],'A responsive environmental adjustment can support access while the teacher learns which conditions allow meaningful participation.','Adjust sensory conditions and monitor participation without stereotyping.','music-volume-access'),
    ],
    'ece-safeguarding-response-and-boundaries'=>[
        $q('easy','A child reports being repeatedly mocked and excluded by classmates because of a disability. Which school response best fits the documented policy scope?','Recognize bullying and discrimination as child-protection concerns and use the school’s applicable protection process.',['Treat the pattern only as a disagreement about game rules.','Wait until the child stops attending the activity before responding.','Ask classmates to decide whether the behavior is serious enough.'],'DepEd Order 40 identifies bullying and discrimination among the harms addressed by the Child Protection Policy.','Recognize bullying and discrimination within child-protection scope.','disability-related-bullying'),
        $q('medium','A learner reports a violent act by an adult in a school setting. Which issue should guide the teacher’s next decision?','The report describes violence, which is within the Child Protection Policy scope, so the teacher should use the applicable school protection process.',['The report is only relevant if it interrupted a lesson.','The teacher should wait for a second report before taking the concern seriously.','The concern should be discussed with classmates before school staff act.'],'The official policy page explicitly includes violence among the harms covered; the school process governs follow-up.','Apply child-protection policy scope to reported violence.','school-violence-report'),
        $q('medium','A staff member says a report of possible exploitation should be handled as a private classroom matter. What is the appropriate professional response?','Recognize exploitation as covered by the Child Protection Policy and use the applicable school protection process.',['Handle the matter privately without referring it through school procedures.','Ignore the concern unless it affects academic performance.','Ask learners to determine whether the event meets a legal definition.'],'DepEd’s policy scope expressly includes exploitation; a teacher should not substitute an informal classroom decision for school procedure.','Recognize exploitation within child-protection scope.','possible-exploitation'),
        $q('medium','A teacher hears that a child was threatened repeatedly by peers. What is the strongest next step?','Treat the recurring threat as a potential violence or bullying concern and follow the school’s applicable child-protection process.',['Classify it as ordinary play without checking the circumstances.','Ask the child to settle the matter alone with the peers.','Wait for the next scheduled class meeting before considering safety.'],'The policy covers violence and bullying; recurring threats warrant the school’s protection pathway rather than an unsupported private judgment.','Identify bullying or violence concerns and use school policy.','repeated-peer-threats'),
        $q('medium','A colleague suggests sharing details of a reported child-protection concern in a general class message so everyone can help. What should the teacher do?','Use the applicable school protection process and avoid placing the concern into a general class communication channel.',['Post the details without the child’s name.','Ask families in the class group to investigate what happened.','Share a summary with learners so they can vote on next steps.'],'The policy concerns serious harms to children; the issue should be handled through the school process rather than a general class channel.','Keep protection concerns within an appropriate school process.','general-class-message'),
        $q('hard','A school activity is planned for young learners, and one proposed task exposes some children to avoidable humiliation. Which professional principle should guide revision?','Protect each child’s rights and equitable participation while keeping the intended learning outcome.',['Keep the task because all learners received the same instructions.','Use public embarrassment to encourage faster participation.','Remove children who may need an adjustment from the activity.'],'The PRC ECE scope includes child rights and equity; protection and participation are part of professionally sound planning.','Apply child-rights and equity principles to early learning.','humiliating-activity-design'),
    ],
    'ece-integrated-developmental-planning'=>[
        $q('easy','A teacher plans a nature walk where children collect leaves, describe their colors, and sort them by shape. Which feature makes this an integrated learning experience?','One shared activity supports observation, oral language, classification, and movement in a meaningful context.',['Plan isolated objectives for each domain even though the walk can connect them through a shared learning task.','The teacher evaluates only how quickly children walk.','The walk replaces observation with a lecture about leaf names.'],'The activity connects several domains through a shared experience and provides observable learning evidence.','Integrate related developmental domains in an intentional activity.','nature-walk-integration'),
        $q('medium','A child is deeply engaged in block construction but has difficulty during a later pencil task about shapes. Which plan best responds to the evidence?','Use blocks to explore and name shape relationships, then observe whether understanding appears in another representation.',['Conclude the child cannot understand shapes because the pencil task was difficult.','Remove all shape vocabulary until the child can complete the worksheet.','Repeat the same pencil task with no change in support.'],'Concrete materials may make the concept accessible; the teacher can use multiple representations and observe transfer.','Connect developmental planning to the child’s demonstrated strengths.','blocks-and-shapes'),
        $q('medium','A class activity includes counting, drawing, and sharing, but the teacher has not decided what evidence to observe. What is the best preparation?','Name the intended learning for each part and select an observable behavior or product that can show it.',['Wait until the activity ends and give one overall participation score.','Use a single age-based milestone to evaluate all parts.','Record only the children who finish first.'],'Assessment should be aligned to the learning intentions and produce usable evidence for planning.','Align observation with explicit developmental learning goals.','integrated-activity-assessment'),
        $q('hard','A teacher must choose the next unit after reviewing observations, work samples, and family input. Which decision process is strongest?','Identify shared learning needs and interests, plan accessible experiences across relevant domains, then monitor how the planned supports work.',['Select the unit that is easiest to prepare, then score children against it.','Plan separate activities based only on each child’s age.','Use family input to replace all curriculum outcomes with unrelated topics.'],'Integrated planning respects evidence and learner context while retaining curriculum goals and checking the effects of support.','Synthesize evidence into a responsive, inclusive plan.','unit-planning-evidence'),
    ],
];
